abstract:
The Nativity was dated to 25 December because that was when the Sun was worshipped and Christians partook of that celebration. An early Roman calendar began in Spring, the time of beginnings in nature; but in 153 BCE it was replaced by 1 January, according to the beginning of the solar year (25 December according to Julius Caesar) (1-3). The council of Tours in 567 declared that the twelve days from Christ’s birth (25 December) to the Epiphany (6 January) should be one long feast; 1 January was made the day of Christ’s Circumcision. While the East and the larger Mediterranean Easter remained the principal Christian festival, the darker and colder north and centre of Europe gravitated towards the winter festivities (3-4). Britain has a number of sun-aligned prehistoric structures, but they are exceptions, so that no overall cult is to be found; moreover, no such structures have been found from the British Iron Age immediately preceding Roman presence (4-5). Irish mythology tells nothing of midwinter practice, though St Patrick suggests that there were sun-worshippers in Ireland. Classical writings do not help. Anglo-Saxon England has Bede, who refers to a prechristian <em>modranicht</em> on 24 December; but Tille argued that Bede may have misunderstood early references to Christmas. <em>Modranicht</em> is not found elsewhere. Christmas was called <em>midne winter</em> or <em>middum wintra</em> until 1038, after which it became <em>Cristes Mæssan</em>. The twelve days of feasting were solidly in place by the time of Alfred’s 877 law code. <em>Yule</em> is an eleventh-century Scandinavian loan (5-6). Snorri claimed that sacrifice was made at the Winter Nights for an easy winter, at Yule for a good crop, and on Summer’s Day (in April) for a good fighting season. <em>Svarfdæla saga</em> chapter 12 has a prechristian warrior delay war until three days after Yule because of the sacral nature of those days [sounds an awful lot like the ?<em>jólahelgi</em> of Christian times]. Still, winter solstice seems to have been a major feast on account of Snorri, Bede, and two dozen complaints by clergymen over revelry (e.g., Wulfstan). In Exeter, New Year’s divination was common practice (7). An early (c7-10) Welsh text speaks of a “New Year feast,” and so do several other Welsh sources. When after 1155 England reverted to presumed Roman practice and began the year on 25 March, celebrations still took place on 31 December and 1 January (8). Chapter four discusses evergreen decoration and the Yule log tradition (probably imported from the Continent). Chapter 17 states that there is no solid evidence for a prechristian spring festival: all we have is Bede. Alfred’s 877 law code made Easter the longest holiday, giving all servants a fortnight off (180). The remainder of the book discusses more recent traditions. Noteworthy:
<ul>
<li>
The early nineteenth century witnessed a severe pruning of Christmas traditions, but abount 1825-50 a sudden revival was responsible for its subsequent popularity (ch. 10);
personifying Christmas was not done until the early seventeenth century (117ff.);
</li><li>
the premodern ploughing season began right after the Christmas holidays, so that the Monday after Twelfth Night was at some point known as Plough Monday; plough races were commonly organised on 7 January (124);
</li><li>
The Old Irish <em>Tochmarc Emire</em> (tenth or eleventh century) mentions the main calendar points, namely the beginning days of the four seasons. Of these, Imbolc is “when the ewes are milked at spring’s beginning” (ch. 12, esp. p. 134; cf. pp. 361, 364-5);
</li><li>
Candlemas, the feast of Mary’s purification and Christ’s recognition as the light of the gentiles, was canonical in England by Bede’s day. While the feast was based in part on the Roman purification rites of February, the candlelit procession seems to have been based on Simeon’s words in the gospel account (139-40);
</li><li>
“The Viking sagas do not seem to preserve a memory of any traditional festival between Yule and the ‘Summer Nights’ in April” (140);
</li><li>
Bede’s <em>Solmonað</em> rituals cannot be demonstrated to form an antecedent to Christian Candlemas (140);
</li><li>
“If its [Candlemas’s] association in the Mediterranean world was chiefly with purification, its importance further north, where winters were darker and colder, was primarily as a celebration of returning light”; the Use of Sarum emphasises this aspect (140);
</li><li>
Shrovetide represented a last opportunity for pleasure or extravagance before Lent, and the obligation to finish stocks of meat, eggs, and cheese; but since provisions at this time of year would have been low, the excess must also have been emotionally welcome. It “marked the end of the chain of cold season celebrations” in high society (151);
</li><li>
In its earliest recorded contexts, <em>lencten</em> always had its double meaning of spring and the fast, and it did not lose its double reference until the seventeenth century. “It was wholly appropriate to a season at which flowers, foliage, warmth, and light were all increasing and yet food and fuel would also be at their shortest.” Most ploughing and sowing took place during Lent (169);
</li><li>
Bede’s poor evidence for the derivation of Easter is discussed; yet cognates are attested throughout Indo-European and are associated with dawn and its goddesses (180). Removing this festival from the canon, there remains no solid evidence for a pre-Christian British festival in March and April; and there may well not have been one, since this was a period of great agricultural activity (180-1);
</li><li>
King Alfred’s two weeks of Easter holiday may be understood as one with a liturgical focus and one of feasting, though the precise boundary between the two varied across time (204);
</li><li>
In early modern times, unusually warm weather during Easter could spark customs otherwise reserved for summer feasts (205);
</li><li>
Two Christmas traditions, blessing the crops and misrule, resurfaced during Easter [in early modern times] (206);
</li><li>
The Irish <em>Sanas Chormaic</em>, (mis)attributed to a ninth-century cleric, records Beltane as an annual fire made by the druids to ward off cattle disease (218);
</li><li>
the bringing in of May was common enough a custom by Robert Grosseteste’s day (c. 1240) that he complained about clerics partaking of it (226);
</li><li>
Pentecost developed as a feast about 305 and grew into a major event by the early fifth century. It involved a procession around the fields to bless the crops; this custom was taken over in England. It was expressly meant to be a solemn, not a festive ritual; the same pattern existed in the Roman Ambarvalia, the feast’s predecessor (277); in the thirteenth century, the processions led to pariochal pride, rivalry, and unruliness (278);
</li><li>
The second half of the calendar years may have had its saints’ days, but it had none of the dramatic communal rituals found between the winter and summer solstices. Corpus Christi was the last of these, but it was instituted late, it arrived in England late and was adopted slowly (304);
</li><li>
There is fourth-century French (Aquitanian) evidence for the custom of rolling a burning wheel downhill to a river; a fourteenth-century English text makes mention of it again, now in England, and early modern evidence suggests it was widespread in northern Europe (311);
</li><li>
A twelfth-century penitential prohibits sorcery at the feast of John the Baptist, and an OE medical text prescribes the picking of vervain at Midsummer Night; “In the northern half of the continent [Europe, it seems] the festival was generally the most important one of the whole year” [ref. to Peter Burke, Popular Culture in Early Modern Europe, 195] (312); 
</li><li>
“The concept of this element [of fire] as the one which purifies and protects seems to be natural to humanity. It is also obvious to a historian of medieval or early modern Europe why late June would be a particularly important time at which to employ it. It preceded the season at which crops would be most vulnerable to weather or blight, and livestock to their diseases. It also ushered in the months in which insects multiplied most widely and in which, therefore, humans were most likely to contract bubonic plague, typhus, and malaria. The fires of St John and St Peter were therefore deployed against serious dangers, and anxieties.” Similarities between the sun and fire are also discussed (321);
</li><li>
“In pre-industrial British society, the cereal harvest, whether of wheat, oats, barley, or rye, represented the most important and most concentrated period of labour in the entire year. It was carried on through the month of August” (332);
</li><li>
July to October implied hard agricultural work, but the weather of this period also facilitated outdoor feasting (348);
</li><li>
<em>Samhain</em> (1 November) has generally been considered the beginning of the Celtic year; it is found as such in early medieval Ireland, mentioned in <em>Tochmarc Emire</em> as the autumn counterpart of <em>Beltane</em>. Situated at the end of the harvest, it provided an ideal time for [political] assemblies, the <em>feis</em> of <em>Samhain</em> lasting a week in the twelfth-century <em>Serglige Con Culaind</em>, which identifies it as “meetings and games and amusements and entertainments and eating and feasting.” Yet nothing is known of its religious/ritual import, possibly because when the texts were drawn up people no longer knew what pagan customs had been (361);
</li><li>
Jeffrey Gantz (Early Irish Myths and Sagas 12-13) has claimed that <em>Samhain</em> was a time especially associated with supernatural powers, since many stories have humans at this feast approached by fairies, monsters, and deities; on the other hand, the assembly might just be a convenient social setting in which to place these tales (361-2);
</li><li>
Bede’s <em>blodmonað</em> cannot be simply dismissed, but how much ritual importance should be awarded to it is open to debate. The Scandinavian <em>vetrnætr</em> are well-documented, but always in Christian times; and whether this festival was brought to Britain is unknown (362);
</li><li>
All Saints started in spring, but was moved to November in the twelfth century. “[T]he withering of flowers and leaves, and the coming of frosts, directed human attention to death and decay” at this time of year (364-5). May Eve and May Day were especially connected with fairy activity in the rural British Isles; it stands to reason that the beginning of winter was much the same, and such traditions are indeed known (365-6);
</li><li>
The slaughtering of animals invoked by Bede’s <em>blodmonað</em> was usually over by the feast of St Martin, which therefore received an air of festivity. It was generally the last feast before the Christmas festivities (386);
</li><li>
The reliability of claims for four Celtic feasts beginning four seasons is drawn into doubt (408-11). Indeed, the opening of the year on 1 November is unproven for both Ireland and Wales, while Welsh material seems to contradict it altogether (410-11);
</li><li>
“Records of seasonal customs in Britain during the early and high Middle Ages do exist, but are so rare that their appearahce can really be described as incidental” (412).
</li>
</ul>
